# Target C-II — phase-zero nesting and a center quantile force a `377`-row adjacent cusp

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only source-level quantitative strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited phase-zero laminar-backbone theorem; audited `0.7404D` weighted smallest-leaf center fan; canonical global strict-rhombus angular-defect budget; exact laminar O--O interval support; KKT interval-switch budget; Hopf--Pannwitz diameter-edge count  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_phase_zero_quantile_cusp_377.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The existing adjacent-cusp proofs first choose one center train and then use the leaf
trains crossed by that center as a complement-dual path.  That step is no longer
necessary after the phase-zero theorem: a positive fraction of the retained
smallest-leaf O--O family is already one nested path before any center train is chosen.

This removes the main path-density loss.  A fixed median cut of the center-phase train
population then supplies the phase control that the old proof obtained from its common
center train.

The result is a substantially smaller simultaneous finite cusp.

## Theorem Q377 — phase-zero quantile cusp

Every unbounded survivor sequence in the stated scope has, for all sufficiently large
members, two consecutive smallest-leaf O--O cuts

\[
\tau_i,\tau_{i+1}
\]

on one nested non-singleton complement-dual path such that

\[
\boxed{N_i\le377,}
\tag{Q377.1}
\]

where

\[
N_i=\#\{h:\varepsilon_{h,i+1}\ne\varepsilon_{hi}\}
\]

is the number of actual diameter rows whose interval incidence switches between the
two cuts.  On the **same pair**,

\[
\boxed{
B_i<{1.16\times10^9\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
}
\tag{Q377.2}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
<{1.16\times10^9\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
}
\tag{Q377.3}
\]

where

\[
B_i=\sum_h\beta_h
|\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi}|.
\]

By the current switching-thrackle theorem, the `<=377` switching diameter edges form a
bipartite caterpillar forest.  All non-switching spanning rows see the usual opposite
shear with two train directions differing by only `O(D^{-1})` projectively.

The large constant in (Q377.2)--(Q377.3) is not intended to be sharp.  The new content
is the discrete support bound `377` while preserving both inverse-`D` currencies on the
same boundary.

---

## 1. Threshold good leaves before the phase-zero split

Use the audited smallest-leaf retained O--O family `Q_V`.  For train `tau`, let
`w_tau in(0,1]` be its retained pair weight and let `d_tau D` be its number of
center-phase strict-rhombus crossings.  The hostile-audited inputs are

\[
{\sum_\tau w_\tau d_\tau\over\sum_\tau w_\tau}
>{1851\over2500}-o(1)=0.7404-o(1),
\tag{Q377.4}
\]

\[
{1\over D}\sum_\tau w_\tau>{53\over625}-o(1)=0.0848-o(1),
\tag{Q377.5}
\]

and

\[
N_C<{1063\over1000}D+o(D)=1.063D+o(D)
\tag{Q377.6}
\]

for the total number of center-phase complete trains.

Choose

\[
\boxed{
\lambda={133\over250}=0.532.
}
\tag{Q377.7}
\]

Call a retained O--O leaf train **good** if

\[
d_\tau>\lambda.
\]

Weighted thresholding gives good retained pair weight

\[
\begin{aligned}
W_g
&>{(0.7404-\lambda)(0.0848)\over1.063-\lambda}D-o(D)\\
&={55226\over1659375}D-o(D)\\
&=0.03328120527\ldots D-o(D).
\end{aligned}
\tag{Q377.8}
\]

Every good train is automatically non-singleton because it crosses at least one center
train in a strict rhombus.

---

## 2. Phase-zero localization makes at least half the good mass one nested path

Apply the hostile-audited phase-zero laminar-backbone mechanism to this **good subset**.
The argument needs only that the selected trains

1. are non-singleton O--O trains;
2. lie in the same shrinking smallest-leaf phase window; and
3. carry the same retained terminal pair weights.

Section 157 puts the smaller retracted normal arc of every selected train within
`o(1)` of one of the two antipodal leaf-phase zero markers.  Endpoint-capacity
domination lets us delete `o(D)` retained pair weight so that each remaining arc
contains its assigned marker strictly in its interior.  Same-phase trains are
pairwise noncrossing, so within either marker class their shorter arcs, and hence their
actual O--O shores, are totally nested.

There are only two marker classes.  Therefore one nested class has retained pair
weight greater than

\[
{W_g\over2}-o(D)
>{27613\over1659375}D-o(D).
\tag{Q377.9}
\]

Since every `w_tau<=1`, this class contains

\[
\boxed{
m>pD-o(D),
\qquad
p={27613\over1659375}
=0.01664060263\ldots .}
\tag{Q377.10}
\]

distinct non-singleton O--O trains.  Order them by nested shore inclusion:

\[
\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m.
\]

This path is obtained **without** choosing a center train that crosses all its leaves.

---

## 3. A fixed median center-phase cut makes every good leaf pay a positive offset

Order all actual center-phase complete trains by their real phase representatives in
the frozen center window.  Split them into lower and upper halves, with the two sizes
differing by at most one.  Choose a real threshold `alpha_0` between the two blocks, so

\[
\alpha_c\le\alpha_0
\quad(c\text{ in the lower block}),
\]

and

\[
\alpha_c\ge\alpha_0
\quad(c\text{ in the upper block}).
\]

Each block contains at most

\[
{N_C\over2}+O(1)
<{1063\over2000}D+o(D)
=0.5315D+o(D)
\tag{Q377.11}
\]

center trains.

A good leaf has more than `0.532D` center neighbors.  Therefore it has more than

\[
\boxed{
d_0D-o(D),
\qquad
d_0={133\over250}-{1063\over2000}
={1\over2000}=0.0005,}
\tag{Q377.12}
\]

neighbors in **each** center block.

Let `beta_tau` be the real phase representative of a good smallest-leaf train.  The
center/leaf phase labels have fixed orientation, so every actual center/leaf crossing
satisfies

\[
\beta_\tau-\alpha_c>{\pi\over3}.
\tag{Q377.13}
\]

Because `tau` has an upper-block neighbor with `alpha_c>=alpha_0`, define

\[
\boxed{
q_\tau:=
\beta_\tau-\alpha_0-{\pi\over3}>0.
}
\tag{Q377.14}
\]

For every lower-block neighbor, `alpha_c<=alpha_0`, so its rhombus angular defect is

\[
\delta_{c\tau}
=\beta_\tau-\alpha_c-{\pi\over3}
=q_\tau+(\alpha_0-\alpha_c)
\ge q_\tau.
\tag{Q377.15}
\]

Thus the median split gives one common phase reference for **all** good leaves, even
though no single center train need cross them all.

---

## 4. Global angular defect bounds total phase offset on the whole nested chain

Every good leaf has at least `d_0D-o(D)` lower-block center neighbors by (Q377.12).
The corresponding center/leaf crossings are distinct strict rhombi.  The canonical
global angular-defect theorem gives

\[
\sum_R\delta_R<C_{phase}D,
\qquad
C_{phase}={\pi^2\sqrt3(2+\sqrt3)\over12}.
\tag{Q377.16}
\]

Summing (Q377.15) first over the lower-block neighbors of each good leaf and then over
all good leaves yields

\[
(d_0D-o(D))\sum_{\tau\ good}q_\tau
<C_{phase}D.
\]
Hence

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{\tau\ good}q_\tau
\le {C_{phase}\over d_0}+o(1).
}
\tag{Q377.17}
\]

The selected nested chain is a subset of the good family, so the same upper bound holds
for its `q_j=q_{tau_j}`.

For adjacent selected leaves, the common reference `alpha_0` cancels:

\[
|\beta_{i+1}-\beta_i|
=|q_{i+1}-q_i|
\le q_i+q_{i+1}.
\tag{Q377.18}
\]

After the frozen phase windows are narrow enough to avoid projective wrap,

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
\le Q_i:=q_i+q_{i+1}.
\tag{Q377.19}
\]

Summing over internal path boundaries,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}Q_i
\le {2C_{phase}\over d_0}+o(1).
}
\tag{Q377.20}
\]

This is the phase budget that replaces the common-center `gamma` argument.

---

## 5. Positive density of boundaries has at most `377` switching rows

Exact laminar interval support is available on the nested path.  Therefore each actual
diameter row starts and stops at most once and

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}N_i
\le2|H|
\le2\pi D.
\tag{Q377.21}
\]

Call a boundary **count-bad** when

\[
N_i\ge378.
\]

Using `pi<355/113`, the count-bad boundary density is at most

\[
{2(355/113)\over378}
={355\over21357}
=0.01662218476\ldots .
\tag{Q377.22}
\]

But the nested-path density in (Q377.10) is

\[
p=0.01664060263\ldots .
\]

The exact certificate proves the positive gap

\[
\boxed{
\rho
:=p-{355\over21357}
={72524\over3937696875}
=1.8417872\ldots\times10^{-5}>0.
}
\tag{Q377.23}
\]

The path has `m-1=pD-o(D)` internal boundaries.  Hence at least

\[
\rho D-o(D)
\]

are count-good.  Since `N_i` is an integer, every count-good boundary has

\[
\boxed{N_i\le377.}
\]

This proves the discrete part of (Q377.1).

---

## 6. Keep beta switching mass and phase gap small on one count-good boundary

For every internal boundary define

\[
B_i=\sum_h\beta_h
|\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi}|.
\]

The positive relative-interior KKT vector is normalized by

\[
\sum_h\beta_h=1.
\]
Since one signed diameter interval switches at most twice,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_iB_i\le2.
}
\tag{Q377.24}
\]

Combine this with (Q377.20):

\[
\sum_iD(B_i+Q_i)
\le
\left(2+{2C_{phase}\over d_0}\right)D+o(D).
\tag{Q377.25}
\]

Restrict the nonnegative sum to the `rho D-o(D)` count-good boundaries.  At least one
of them satisfies

\[
D(B_i+Q_i)
\le
{2+2C_{phase}/d_0\over\rho}+o(1).
\tag{Q377.26}
\]

Using the same outward enclosure employed by the current adjacent-cut certificates,

\[
C_{phase}
<{3258689123681\over612912000000},
\]

the exact verifier proves

\[
{2+2C_{phase}^{up}/d_0\over\rho}
<1.16\times10^9.
\tag{Q377.27}
\]

Both `B_i` and `Q_i` are nonnegative.  Thus the selected count-good boundary satisfies
(Q377.2), while (Q377.19) gives (Q377.3).

---

## 7. Interaction with the current switching normal form

For any adjacent nested O--O cuts, the switching rows are exactly the actual-diameter
graph cut across the intervening vertex layer:

\[
H_{sw}=\delta_H(L).
\]

The actual diameter graph is a straight-line thrackle.  The current switching theorem
therefore makes `H_sw` a bipartite caterpillar forest.  Applying it to the pair above
gives the finite normal form

```text
<=377 switching actual diameter edges
+ one layer-cut caterpillar forest
+ total switching beta mass O(1/D)
+ adjacent train phase gap O(1/D)
+ exact two-cut O-O multishear available
```

This improves the discrete support size without changing the hostile-audited warning:
small KKT mass does not itself remove an active max-function cusp, and evaluating the
finite multishear at parameter scale `1/D` is not yet a uniformly certified feasible
unit-separated deformation because inactive noncontact slack has no uniform floor.

---

## 8. Why the center quantile removes the old path-extraction loss

The logical difference from the existing common-center backbone is worth isolating.

Old route:

```text
weighted fan
 -> good leaves
 -> choose top center block
 -> average to one center gamma
 -> leaves crossed by gamma form a path
 -> gamma provides the common phase reference
```

New route:

```text
weighted fan
 -> good leaves
 -> Section-157 phase-zero split
 -> one half of the good pair mass is already a nested path
 -> median center phase alpha_0 is a common reference
 -> every good leaf hits both center halves
 -> lower-half rhombi pay q_tau
```

The phase-zero geometry supplies the path, while the center quantile supplies the phase
reference.  They no longer need to be supplied by the same center train.  That is the
source of the factor-of-more-than-two improvement in usable path density.

---

## 9. Scope

- The theorem inherits the proof-only status of the phase-zero laminar-backbone result.
- Every selected good train is non-singleton because its center degree is positive.
- The marker-neighborhood/tied-side deletion costs only `o(D)` retained pair weight and
  is already included in the phase-zero hostile audit.
- The median split uses actual center trains, not a continuum distribution; the `O(1)`
  parity/tie error is absorbed into `o(D)`.
- No individual KKT coefficient floor is used.
- No local descent or same-cardinality replacement is claimed.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only phase-zero/center-quantile strengthening.  C-II and
Level I remain open.
